Z||000000||Formula88||03-10-2001||09:45 PM||||The 3 week clutch swap fiasco has sucessfully concluded!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>What started out at needing a clutch, turned into a major hassle when the input bearing sleeve was broken off.  Ok, order one from the Fiero Store, 2nd Day Air.  UPS looses the shipment.  1 week later I get my part. (shipping refunded, without having to ask, btw).  Next comes the task of getting the old bearing out and putting the new one in.  Being a bit daunted at the thought of splitting the case on my tranny, I try the dremel method to cut out the bearing.  After several hours and lots of cussing later, I gave up and cracked the case using Rodney's basic instructions.  WOW.  That was SO much easier than I expected.  And definately easier than cutting the bearing out.  $10 and 10 minutes at the machine shop had the new bearing in.  Had the tranny back together at 2pm today, and the car on the road by 9pm.    That RAM clutch seems to grab much harder than the original clutch, but that could just be break in.  Hard to tell.  Anyway, my baby is on the road again and I'd like to formally thank the following people:<P>Rodney Dickman for his Getrag instructions.<BR>The dude at Advance auto who hooked me up with the sealant for the tranny.<BR>The dude at CarQuest's machine shop who stopped what he was doing 10 minutes before close and did my bearing for me.<BR>Dan Bliley, for advising me of the only machine shop open on Saturday in the area.<P>The Fiero Store for my clutch, input shaft bearing, and some of the best customer service I've seen.<P>And most all, Pat Heinske a.k.a. LZeitgeist, whithout his wrenching and expertise, this job could not have been done.  <P>Thank you all!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
